=== (4) loop-weighted anomaly, d = 1 and d = 3 (separable NN fine lattice, boost along x) ===
   R_s(N) = sum A_x w^s / sum w^s over all modes of the fine zone (= all N^d bands x coarse zone)
   abs = sum_modes A_x w/2 per coarse cell, divided by N^(d+1) (natural size of a cutoff-N vacuum loop)
  d = 1
     N     R_1       R_-1      R_-3      R_-5      abs/N^(d+1)
      4   -1.3083   -0.5653   -0.0752   -0.0161   -0.8586
      8   -1.3252   -0.4778   -0.0245   -0.0040   -0.8513
     16   -1.3308   -0.4114   -0.0075   -0.0010   -0.8494
     32   -1.3326   -0.3605   -0.0022   -0.0002   -0.8490
     64   -1.3331   -0.3205   -0.0006   -0.0001   -0.8489
    128   -1.3333   -0.2885   -0.0002   -0.0000   -0.8488
    256   -1.3333   -0.2623   -0.0001   -0.0000   -0.8488
  d = 3
     N     R_1       R_-1      R_-3      R_-5      abs/N^(d+1)
      2   -1.0873   -0.8901   -0.5685   -0.2179   -1.3279
      4   -1.0908   -0.8798   -0.4651   -0.0820   -1.3095
      8   -1.0917   -0.8758   -0.3875   -0.0268   -1.3049
     16   -1.0920   -0.8745   -0.3300   -0.0081   -1.3037
     32   -1.0920   -0.8740   -0.2866   -0.0024   -1.3034
     64   -1.0921   -0.8739   -0.2532   -0.0007   -1.3033
-> UV-dominated loop weights (zero-point energy, tadpole) carry an N-independent O(1) relative anomaly;
   the marginal weight s = -d falls only like 1/ln N; IR-dominated weights (s < -d) fall like a power of N.
   The absolute vacuum anomaly per coarse cell grows like N^(d+1) (abs/N^(d+1) -> constant).
